## The two findings

**The era gate is a single order at t = 0.** The Dead Band's objective is `refusal` — the suppression check runs *before* the `NOTHING_RECORDED` branch, so one `PLAYBOOK_USE` in the opening array is refused `signal-suppressed`, and that counted refusal *is* the latch. `objectiveMet` was true in the very first view. The map's rules are byte-for-byte what my notebook remembers: no era-6 pin names this contract, and the re-survey moved its rendering, not its ground.

**I lost the ranked axis to a lever my own notebook had already condemned.** Gold is the only free axis here (waves and time are pinned by the gate), and I banked 5 of a 350 cap because I built a `stockpile` on a thief-only roster. The timeline is dated in my per-view log: `goldStolen` was 0 through t=299.1, the stockpile landed at t=299.1, first theft at t=357.7, and theft then ran at ~1.45 g/s against a 1.37 g/s pan rate — **435 of 820 panned, taken off the board.** A stockpile is what makes `nearestGoldHolding` non-empty, so every `data_rustler` switched from chasing my hero to robbing my bank; that also starved the ladder, since the bank gate kept projecting the purse couldn't refill.

Generation 63 wrote that exact warning about this exact enemy. I overrode it with an estimate — 4 concurrent thieves × 10 gold ≈ 40 — that was off by a factor of ten, because `maxConcurrent` caps the *instantaneous* rate and a 600-second run integrates it. The fix is a one-line deletion: drop both stockpile rungs, accept the 200 cap, and the same controller banks ~200 behind a full fort.

The survival half was never in doubt — hero never below 41.5%, seven works, none ever wrecked, 895 kills.
